WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.618
Picture the doctor from last time, now looking at a clear board.

2
00:00:06.618 --> 00:00:09.433
It says bed four got worse at fourteen oh two.

3
00:00:09.633 --> 00:00:11.618
Useful, but it is not the story.

4
00:00:11.618 --> 00:00:17.199
The story is in the nurses' notes: what the patient said, what was given, what went wrong.

5
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:22.932
But each ward keeps its notes in its own drawer, and the drawer is emptied when the patient leaves.

6
00:00:23.133 --> 00:00:24.929
Our app has the same drawer.

7
00:00:24.929 --> 00:00:31.148
docker compose logs shows the app's logs, the lines a program writes as it works, on one machine.

8
00:00:31.148 --> 00:00:33.841
Remove the container, and they go with it.

9
00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:38.404
With ten servers, you would search in ten places, at 3 in the morning.

10
00:00:39.233 --> 00:00:42.886
So we want one place for the logs of every container.

11
00:00:42.886 --> 00:00:45.918
That place is Loki, Grafana Labs' log store.

12
00:00:45.918 --> 00:00:49.571
To find lines fast, it keeps an index, a lookup list.

13
00:00:49.571 --> 00:00:50.673
What goes in it?

14
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:53.736
Let's be fair to B.

15
00:00:53.736 --> 00:00:55.882
Every word, like a search engine.

16
00:00:55.882 --> 00:00:59.133
That sounds like the fastest way to find anything.

17
00:01:02.667 --> 00:01:04.979
Picture a hospital records office.

18
00:01:04.979 --> 00:01:10.147
It files notes by ward, bed and date, and never catalogues the words inside.

19
00:01:10.367 --> 00:01:13.296
The folders sit on the shelves, full of pages.

20
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:19.416
To find a phrase, a clerk looks up bed four on Monday, opens just that folder, and reads.

21
00:01:19.633 --> 00:01:21.157
The catalogue stays tiny.

22
00:01:21.157 --> 00:01:24.447
And the reading is quick, because the folders are few.

23
00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:25.761
That is Loki.

24
00:01:25.761 --> 00:01:27.107
The answer is A.

25
00:01:27.107 --> 00:01:29.380
It indexes only the labels.

26
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:34.665
Each log line carries a few labels, a name and a value, such as service is web.

27
00:01:34.665 --> 00:01:37.485
The catalogue holds those, and nothing else.

28
00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:41.950
All lines with exactly the same labels form one log stream.

29
00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:43.175
That is a folder.

30
00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:47.951
The text of each line is packed tight, compressed, and not indexed at all.

31
00:01:48.167 --> 00:01:49.117
So why not B?

32
00:01:49.117 --> 00:01:56.204
A store that indexes every word finds a word across all logs faster, but pays in disk and memory.

33
00:01:56.204 --> 00:01:58.469
Loki chose the small catalogue.

34
00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.386
Someone has to carry the notes to the office.

35
00:02:02.386 --> 00:02:06.287
A log has no current value to scrape; it is a stream of events.

36
00:02:06.287 --> 00:02:09.135
So a collector sends the lines as they appear.

37
00:02:09.367 --> 00:02:12.061
In the lab that porter is Grafana Alloy.

38
00:02:12.061 --> 00:02:17.785
It finds each container, labels it, follows its output, and pushes the lines to Loki.

39
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:24.705
Older tutorials use Promtail, which reached end of life on the second of March, 2026.

40
00:02:24.933 --> 00:02:28.332
And Grafana asks Loki, right next to Prometheus.

41
00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:30.996
Now let's ask the office.

42
00:02:30.996 --> 00:02:35.168
Loki's query language, LogQL, borrows a lot from PromQL.

43
00:02:35.168 --> 00:02:39.265
Braces pick the folders: here, every line from the app.

44
00:02:39.467 --> 00:02:42.186
Then a pipe, and steps that read inside them.

45
00:02:42.186 --> 00:02:46.477
First, drop every line that contains slash health Z, the health checks.

46
00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:54.365
Next, json: read each line's names and values, so method, path and status become labels for this query.

47
00:02:54.567 --> 00:02:58.755
Then keep only the failed requests: status five hundred or more.

48
00:02:58.967 --> 00:03:02.387
And rewrite each line in a shape that is easy to scan.

49
00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:03.690
Notice the order.

50
00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:05.807
The plain text filter came first.

51
00:03:05.807 --> 00:03:11.452
It is the cheapest step, and every line it drops is a line the parser never has to read.

52
00:03:12.567 --> 00:03:15.155
LogQL can also turn lines into numbers.

53
00:03:15.155 --> 00:03:21.327
Count the requests by status over five minutes, and graph them next to the Prometheus panels.

54
00:03:21.533 --> 00:03:26.883
But when Redis is down, the app prints a Python traceback, lines that are not JSON.

55
00:03:26.883 --> 00:03:29.589
The parser marks them with an error label.

56
00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:34.905
A metric query refuses to run until you drop them, with the empty error label.

57
00:03:35.633 --> 00:03:37.711
Now let's break it on purpose.

58
00:03:37.711 --> 00:03:42.004
Today the catalogue is tiny: a few services, a few containers.

59
00:03:42.233 --> 00:03:48.324
Someone thinks: let's add the request ID as a label, so we can find any request instantly.

60
00:03:48.324 --> 00:03:50.152
What happens to the office?

61
00:03:50.152 --> 00:03:51.235
Pause and guess.

62
00:03:53.467 --> 00:03:57.557
Every new label value makes a new stream: one folder per request.

63
00:03:57.557 --> 00:04:00.200
The catalogue grows bigger than the notes.

64
00:04:00.433 --> 00:04:03.619
Well, that is one way to make a fast system slow.

65
00:04:04.333 --> 00:04:11.066
So the labels are for things with a few values: service, environment, cluster, container.

66
00:04:11.066 --> 00:04:14.395
Never request IDs, user IDs or IP addresses.

67
00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:18.525
Keep those values in the line, where a text filter finds them.

68
00:04:18.525 --> 00:04:23.083
Loki's docs suggest ten to fifteen labels at most, each with few values.

69
00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:24.953
One honest limit.

70
00:04:24.953 --> 00:04:28.277
Loki is fast because it reads only a few folders.

71
00:04:28.277 --> 00:04:32.618
To find one word across every log at once, it is the wrong tool.

72
00:04:32.618 --> 00:04:36.349
The next lesson brings a store that indexes every word.

73
00:04:37.067 --> 00:04:38.980
So the moral of the story.

74
00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:40.746
Labels pick the folders.

75
00:04:40.746 --> 00:04:42.292
The text stays cheap.

76
00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:45.231
One place for every container's lines.

77
00:04:45.231 --> 00:04:47.531
The catalogue holds only labels.

78
00:04:47.531 --> 00:04:50.908
LogQL picks folders, filters text, then parses.

79
00:04:50.908 --> 00:04:53.855
And IDs go in the line, never in a label.

80
00:04:54.567 --> 00:04:55.508
A quick check.

81
00:04:55.508 --> 00:05:00.415
Which reads less: the web service's lines, filtered for the word timeout?

82
00:05:00.415 --> 00:05:03.440
Or the same filter over every service's logs?

83
00:05:06.567 --> 00:05:07.260
The first.

84
00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:10.932
The labels pick one folder before any reading starts.
